---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseStudyProse from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyProse.astro";
import CaseStudySection from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudySection.astro";
import CaseStudyCards from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCards.astro";
import CaseStudyCard from "../../components/case-study/CaseStudyCard.astro";

import heroImage from "../../assets/images/product-data-pipeline/converter.png";
import reportImage from "../../assets/images/product-data-pipeline/report.png";

const versions = [
	{
		title: "Python script (2022)",
		text: "Written by hand with pandas and run from the command line. It worked well for me, but nobody else could use it.",
	},
	{
		title: "React app",
		text: "An early experiment using AI to turn the script into a web app the team could use. It proved the idea, but it was buggy and not fit for everyday use.",
	},
	{
		title: "Plain JavaScript app",
		text: "Rewritten by hand without React, which was overkill for the job. I wanted to understand every line, which matters when AI is helping to write code.",
	},
	{
		title: "TypeScript (2026)",
		text: "The current version: the JavaScript app refined, typed and extended with AI assistance, into a polished tool ready for production.",
	},
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="Building the product data pipeline"
	pageTitle="Product data pipeline case study - Will Jackson"
	description="How I turned a hand-written Python script into a browser-based tool that converts one product spreadsheet into data for the CRM, the website and showroom tickets."
	path="/work/product-data-pipeline/"
	lede="One product spreadsheet now feeds the CRM, the website and the showroom tickets, through a browser-based tool that anyone on the team can use. Updating a brand went from most of a working day to an hour or two."
	facts={[
		{ label: "Client", value: "Woven & Woods, flooring showrooms in Twickenham and Richmond" },
		{ label: "Role", value: "Design and development" },
		{ label: "Stack", value: "TypeScript, Vite, SheetJS, Tailwind CSS, Dokploy" },
		{ label: "Year", value: "2022 to present" },
	]}
	heroImage={heroImage}
	heroImageAlt="The Product Data Converter, with a spreadsheet selected and Website XML chosen as the output"
	link="https://data.wovenandwoods.com"
	linkText="Visit the tool"
>
	<CaseStudyProse heading="The brief">
		<p>
			Woven & Woods sells 800+ ranges from more than 60 manufacturers, and
			their details and prices change all the time. Every update used to be
			copied by hand from a spreadsheet into Simpro, the website and the
			showroom ticket templates, which took most of a working day per
			brand.
		</p>
		<p>
			Copying by hand also caused errors. Each of those systems relies on
			its column headings being exactly right, so a single spelling
			mistake or a capital letter in the wrong place meant a field
			silently wasn't recognised. The fix needed to force every update
			into the same structure, and work for my colleague, the commercial
			manager, as well as for me.
		</p>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudySection
		heading="One spreadsheet, four outputs"
		image={reportImage}
		imageAlt="A conversion report showing rows processed, ranges processed, skipped rows and a warning for an invalid category on row 8"
	>
		<p>
			You upload a spreadsheet, choose an output and download the
			result. The tool produces a CSV for Simpro, with generated search
			terms and notes for the sales team, ticket-ready data for the
			showroom printers, and XML and JSON for the current and new versions
			of the website, both imported with WP All Import.
		</p>
		<p>
			Along the way it recognises older column names, skips discontinued
			products, groups colours into ranges and checks every row. Problem
			rows are skipped with a warning that gives the row number, like the
			misspelt category in this example, and a report shows exactly what
			was converted.
		</p>
	</CaseStudySection>

	<CaseStudyProse heading="Four versions, each simpler and sturdier">
		<p>
			The tool has been rebuilt three times as I learned what the team
			actually needed:
		</p>
		<ol class="space-y-4">
			{
				versions.map((version, index) => (
					<li class="grid gap-1 sm:grid-cols-[2rem_1fr]">
						<span class="font-heading font-bold text-teal-900">
							{index + 1}.
						</span>
						<span>
							<strong class="text-neutral-950">
								{version.title}.
							</strong>{" "}
							{version.text}
						</span>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ol>
	</CaseStudyProse>

	<CaseStudyCards>
		<CaseStudyCard heading="Built for the whole team">
			<p>
				I use Ubuntu and the commercial manager uses macOS, and Python
				scripts were never going to work for someone who isn't
				technical. I considered a desktop app built with Rust and Tauri,
				but a web app could run on Windows, macOS and Linux and build on
				the HTML and TypeScript I already knew.
			</p>
			<p>
				Spreadsheets are processed entirely in the browser and never
				uploaded, so cost prices stay on the user's own computer. The
				tool is hosted with Dokploy.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>

		<CaseStudyCard heading="Using AI without losing control">
			<p>
				The first AI-assisted version taught me that working code isn't
				enough if I can't follow it. Since then I've used AI to speed up
				work on code I understand and can maintain myself, rather than to
				write code for me.
			</p>
			<p>
				The ticket export also powers the showroom's automated price
				tickets, so a price change now reaches the CRM, the website and
				the shop floor from one place.
			</p>
		</CaseStudyCard>
	</CaseStudyCards>
</CaseStudyLayout>
